WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.320
Okay, so now let's keep moving.

00:05.360 --> 00:09.480
Now we can talk about the parameters inside stored procedures.

00:09.480 --> 00:10.840
So what is a parameter.

00:11.000 --> 00:16.760
It is like a placeholder where you can pass in information from you into the stored procedure while

00:16.760 --> 00:17.440
running it.

00:17.440 --> 00:23.600
And using parameters in stored procedure is going to make it flexible, reusable and dynamic.

00:23.600 --> 00:25.320
So let's understand what this means.

00:25.480 --> 00:27.880
Let's say that you got a new task.

00:27.880 --> 00:33.080
So it says for German customers find the total number of customers and the average score.

00:33.120 --> 00:37.520
So that means now you have like to generate two reports one for USA and one for Germany.

00:37.520 --> 00:39.960
And in both of them you are doing the same aggregation.

00:40.000 --> 00:42.360
And again we have to go and start writing the query.

00:42.360 --> 00:45.920
It's going to be very similar to the one that we have in the previous example.

00:45.920 --> 00:48.400
So we are doing the same stuff, same aggregations.

00:48.400 --> 00:53.120
But the only change here is that we're going to use another value to filter the data.

00:53.120 --> 00:56.520
So instead of USA we're going to go and say here Germany.

00:56.520 --> 00:58.880
So let's go and execute this one over here.

00:58.880 --> 01:01.550
And with that we can see we have total number of customers two.

01:01.590 --> 01:04.990
So this is the report that we have to provide like in weekly basis.

01:04.990 --> 01:10.030
And again in order not to go and copy paste stuff, we're going to go and create a stored procedure

01:10.030 --> 01:10.830
for that.

01:10.870 --> 01:12.510
At the end you can have an end.

01:12.510 --> 01:15.030
But now of course we cannot have like the same names.

01:15.030 --> 01:17.350
We're going to go and say here Germany.

01:17.350 --> 01:19.510
So let's go and execute it.

01:19.510 --> 01:23.790
And the next step we have to go and execute the stored procedure.

01:23.790 --> 01:26.950
So like this let's go and execute it.

01:26.950 --> 01:29.870
And the whole logic is now stored inside the database.

01:29.870 --> 01:32.670
Let's go and refresh on the explorer over here.

01:32.670 --> 01:35.150
And you can see now we have two stored procedures.

01:35.150 --> 01:37.310
But now you have to feel there is something wrong.

01:37.350 --> 01:39.390
Always in programming and coding.

01:39.670 --> 01:45.910
If you find yourself repeating the same task over and over, then there is always a smarter way on how

01:45.910 --> 01:47.070
to optimize that.

01:47.110 --> 01:49.870
Repeating stuff in coding is always a bad thing.

01:49.870 --> 01:54.430
So now clearly we are repeating the same query in two different stored procedure.

01:54.470 --> 01:58.050
And now if you compare them, you see it's because of the value.

01:58.050 --> 02:01.610
So we have here the value for the filter once Germany and one USA.

02:01.650 --> 02:03.690
And those values are static values.

02:03.690 --> 02:07.010
So it's always going to stay inside the stored procedure as USA.

02:07.050 --> 02:11.730
But instead of that we can replace those static values with a parameter.

02:11.730 --> 02:17.650
And then you decide as you are executing the stored procedure for which country you want to execute

02:17.650 --> 02:18.890
the stored procedure.

02:18.890 --> 02:20.210
So let's go and do that.

02:20.210 --> 02:25.170
I'm just going to remove everything from here and focus only on the first stored procedure.

02:25.210 --> 02:32.050
Now what we're going to do after giving it a name of our stored procedure we have to define our parameter.

02:32.050 --> 02:33.610
So it starts with ads.

02:33.650 --> 02:35.010
And with that SQL understand.

02:35.050 --> 02:35.210
Aha.

02:35.330 --> 02:36.850
Now we are talking about parameters.

02:36.850 --> 02:38.930
And we need now the name of the parameter.

02:38.930 --> 02:40.690
So it's going to be country.

02:40.690 --> 02:42.250
It could be any name that you want.

02:42.290 --> 02:45.330
And after that we have to define for SQL the data type.

02:45.330 --> 02:50.450
It's like when you are creating a table and you define columns you assign a data type for each column.

02:50.450 --> 02:54.330
The same thing here you have to assign as well a data type for each parameter.

02:54.330 --> 02:56.960
So we're going to use the data datatype in Varchar.

02:57.280 --> 03:00.480
And for the countries it's enough to have the links of 50.

03:00.520 --> 03:06.120
So with that, we are telling SQL for this stored procedure, we can pass an information to the stored

03:06.120 --> 03:06.720
procedure.

03:06.720 --> 03:10.400
And this information and value can be used inside this parameter.

03:10.440 --> 03:16.720
So now after we defined this parameter over here we can go and use it anywhere inside our query.

03:16.760 --> 03:20.320
And of course we want to go and use it instead of this static value.

03:20.480 --> 03:22.800
So now we're going to remove this static value.

03:22.800 --> 03:24.840
And instead we can have the parameter.

03:24.840 --> 03:30.520
So now we are saying you're going to filter the table based on the value that comes from the user.

03:30.520 --> 03:32.440
And not any more static with the USA.

03:32.480 --> 03:37.240
And as I said you can use this parameter everywhere like even here in the Select statements.

03:37.240 --> 03:40.200
So it is a value that could be used everywhere in your query.

03:40.200 --> 03:40.800
So that's it.

03:40.840 --> 03:46.080
We have defined our new parameter and we have used this parameter in our query.

03:46.080 --> 03:48.640
So now we have to go and update the stored procedure.

03:48.640 --> 03:50.400
We cannot leave it as create.

03:50.440 --> 03:52.320
Instead of that we're going to say alter.

03:52.320 --> 03:57.980
So we are saying alter stored procedure, and with the new information, let's go and execute it.

03:57.980 --> 04:00.140
And now we have to go and execute it.

04:00.140 --> 04:03.380
So now what we're going to do we're going to say execute get customer summary.

04:03.380 --> 04:07.420
But now our stored procedure is expecting a value from you from the input.

04:07.420 --> 04:10.260
So we're going to do it exactly like we've done in the name over here.

04:10.580 --> 04:16.260
So we're going to say the parameter country is equal to Germany.

04:16.260 --> 04:20.500
So that means the value of this parameter come from me come from the input.

04:20.500 --> 04:24.380
And this information can be passed to my query to the stored procedure.

04:24.580 --> 04:26.340
So let's go and execute it.

04:26.340 --> 04:30.580
And with that as you can see we are getting the report of customers for Germany.

04:30.620 --> 04:36.900
And now if you say okay let's go and generate the report for USA, all that you have to do is that replace

04:36.900 --> 04:37.700
the parameter.

04:37.700 --> 04:40.980
So in the value instead of Germany we're going to say USA.

04:41.220 --> 04:43.180
So let's go and execute it.

04:43.220 --> 04:43.580
Great.

04:43.620 --> 04:47.100
Now we are getting as well the report for us customers.

04:47.100 --> 04:52.610
So that means my friends for those two reports I just need one stored procedure and with the help of

04:52.610 --> 04:57.170
the parameter, I made my stored procedure now more flexible and professional.

04:57.170 --> 04:59.890
So this is exactly the power of the parameters.

05:00.130 --> 05:03.450
It makes everything reusable and dynamic.

05:03.490 --> 05:07.130
And now of course we don't need the stored procedure for Germany.

05:07.130 --> 05:09.090
So what we can do we can go and drop it.

05:09.370 --> 05:11.970
So we're going to say drop procedure.

05:12.170 --> 05:15.090
And it was like this Germany.

05:15.090 --> 05:17.490
So we don't need this stored procedure.

05:17.490 --> 05:20.890
And we're going to stay with only one dynamic stored procedure.

05:20.890 --> 05:24.650
So this is how to use parameters in stored procedure and why it's important.

05:24.690 --> 05:24.970
Okay.

05:25.010 --> 05:30.210
So now to the next step is that we can go and add default values for the parameters.

05:30.290 --> 05:35.090
So let's say that I execute very frequently this report where I say the country equal to USA.

05:35.370 --> 05:39.290
And I don't want each time to define the parameter value equal to USA.

05:39.530 --> 05:45.090
So if you are using a value very frequently, you can add it as a default inside the definition of the

05:45.090 --> 05:46.010
stored procedure.

05:46.010 --> 05:47.410
And it is very simple.

05:47.410 --> 05:53.190
So if you go to the definition again over here after the parameter and you say equal to USA.

05:53.910 --> 05:58.870
So now it's very important to understand that the country will not be always equal to USA.

05:59.030 --> 06:04.830
It is just you are saying if I don't get from the user any value, then as a default I'm going to go

06:04.830 --> 06:06.150
and use the USA.

06:06.430 --> 06:11.310
So let's go and again change the definition of our stored procedure using alter.

06:11.550 --> 06:12.470
So execute.

06:12.510 --> 06:14.590
And now we can go to our stored procedure.

06:14.590 --> 06:18.190
And I can skip the whole thing over here and execute it.

06:18.230 --> 06:23.750
So now as a default I'm getting the report of USA without passing any information to the stored procedure

06:23.750 --> 06:26.070
because I know it is as a default USA.

06:26.390 --> 06:30.430
But if you need it as a Germany, of course you have to go and define it.

06:30.430 --> 06:34.350
So you say execute the stored procedure where the country equal to Germany.

06:34.510 --> 06:37.630
So if I execute it like this SQL is still going to use your value.

06:37.670 --> 06:43.390
So the value that comes as an input from the user has more priority of course as the default.

06:43.430 --> 06:45.710
And with that we are getting the Germany reports.

06:45.750 --> 06:47.230
So as you can see it's really nice right.

06:47.230 --> 06:49.230
Using parameters in stored procedure.
